<article class="env-lemma" id="05SI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05SI">Lemma <span data-tag="05SI">12.19.4</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category. Let $f : A \to B$ be a morphism of filtered objects of $\mathcal{A}$. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is strict, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the morphism $\mathop{\mathrm{Coim}}(f) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(f)$ of Lemma <a href="/tag/0107" data-tag="0107">12.3.12</a> is an isomorphism. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Note that $\mathop{\mathrm{Coim}}(f) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(f)$ is an isomorphism of objects of $\mathcal{A}$, and that part (2) signifies that it is an isomorphism of filtered objects. By the description of kernels and cokernels in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/0122" data-tag="0122">12.19.2</a> we see that the filtration on $\mathop{\mathrm{Coim}}(f)$ is the quotient filtration coming from $A \to \mathop{\mathrm{Coim}}(f)$. Similarly, the filtration on $\mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(f)$ is the induced filtration coming from the injection $\mathop{\mathrm{Im}}(f) \to B$. The definition of strict is exactly that the quotient filtration is the induced filtration. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>